[{:db/id "q-334645/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "7f9bfed9-6016-5ea2-bff0-f2d0a363d5db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334645/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "173db8f7-a715-5a70-a359-ff61ba75d31a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334645/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "a0487951-0215-5ba5-aaae-47f271b10528" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334645/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "304e1c61-b18a-5cc8-bbde-10cb0e711781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334645/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "32c0d0ef-8149-5f2a-bb97-be3f1037476c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334645/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1eb6629-9439-5577-b4ff-137e6f6c8e2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334645/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-334645/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-334645/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334645/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-334645/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-334645/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334645" :question/id #uuid "792e1f89-f2c3-5839-895c-6eb10fc13178" :question/math-academy-id "q-334645" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.16,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.16)=0.40.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.04,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.04)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.16$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.16}{0.04}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.40$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.40}{0.005}=80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.161… \\\\ p & ≈2.161….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334645/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334645"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334647/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "8953ec9f-5675-5bc4-95c3-3798aef841f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334647/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "ebde9b7c-9901-5cdf-9732-773857fc0167" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334647/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "54489bf9-5fc9-58a6-80ba-096dff2ab70a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334647/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "6e6aba09-ca9c-54bf-970a-5772b1b3dcbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334647/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "131c99ee-42c2-5fd2-a654-1742675953d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334647/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7eebf7f8-1795-5cfe-ae23-6bcc181442dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334647/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-334647/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-334647/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-334647/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334647/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334647/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334647" :question/id #uuid "a2ce911a-8124-5a66-812b-ed79e2f51c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-334647" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.15,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.15)=0.54.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.003.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.05}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.54$ to $0.003.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.54}{0.003}=180.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈180 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(180) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.726… \\\\ p & ≈3.726….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334647/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334647"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334753/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3d538766-072c-5796-ac87-dab54b04e164" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334753/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "52ca5300-a24a-51d9-ae3d-fba9fbf488bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334753/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "fba7862b-16e7-5d45-ace7-46229633ea7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334753/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "9f4f43f4-400d-5ca2-8008-3681f9b38f15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334753/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "c2eafb80-10b8-5c87-8b44-1dc304f7126c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334753/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "92178e62-4839-510e-bdbb-8210fc101bc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334753/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-334753/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-334753/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-334753/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-334753/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-334753/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334753" :question/id #uuid "f0bdeb7f-c72e-54d6-b6ac-05f132bb2bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334753" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{3}=0.3(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{5}=9(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334753/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334753"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334756/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "4b1dce58-ba5e-5ecd-9da7-1f506c957a29" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334756/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b4930550-2c52-5b12-9884-97e54135e4ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334756/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a2ccfa72-c444-53c8-8489-a6f5ff529e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334756/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "77bf41e8-3f2f-5c35-85fb-e8c0ff8298b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334756/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1c5dc261-951f-58e4-ac81-9cfc8e8e0563" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334756/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "22df5fc2-30a5-5903-88d4-35266894a4aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334756/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-334756/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-334756/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-334756/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-334756/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-334756/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334756" :question/id #uuid "c07fc2d9-cd40-5751-8c9f-7d5d62132eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-334756" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-334756/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334756"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334951/field-1/2df49bfd2916621cd980c1f63441e883aae9a0517080d4c44541374d5161fe20" :answer/id #uuid "6ad43846-bee9-5c21-b5fe-83afbabe445d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334951/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "54f51b0b-b0a6-5a71-8620-163ab26d94f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334951/field-1/2df49bfd2916621cd980c1f63441e883aae9a0517080d4c44541374d5161fe20" :answer-field/choices ["q-334951/field-1/2df49bfd2916621cd980c1f63441e883aae9a0517080d4c44541374d5161fe20"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334951" :question/id #uuid "7cc8c4d7-f786-5b1c-a1c0-7482e91fbcb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334951" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.24$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.24)≈0.32.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.12,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.12)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.12.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.24}{0.12}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3+1}={2}^{4}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.12,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.12)≈\\frac{0.32}{16}=0.02.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334951/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334951"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
